1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 f5 4. d3 Nf6 5. O-O fxe4 6. dxe4 d6 7. Nc3 Be7 8. h3
O-O 9. Ng5 (9. Bc4+ {and 10 a3 might be suggested}) 9... Qe8 10. f4 (10. Bc4+ {
10 and a3}) 10... h6 11. Bc4+ Kh8 12. Ne6 Bxe6 13. Bxe6 Nd4 14. Bb3 Nxb3 15.
axb3 Qc6 16. Qe2 (16. Qd3) 16... exf4 17. Bxf4 a6 18. Rae1 Rae8 19. Qd3 Nd7 20.
Nd5 Nc5 21. Qc3 Ne6 ({Black could have ventured here on a less complicated
variation, with} 21... Nxe4 22. Nxe7 Rxe7 {etc, with a number of possibilities
without incurring any risk}) 22. Qd2 Nxf4 23. Nxf4 Qc5+ 24. Kh1 Bg5 25. Ng6+
Kg8 26. Qd3 Rxf1+ 27. Rxf1 Bf6 28. e5 dxe5 29. b4 Qc6 (29... Qxb4 30. Qd5+ Kh7
31. Rxf6 $1) 30. Qb3+ Qe6 31. Qg3 Bg5 32. Nh4 Bxh4 33. Qxh4 e4 34. Re1 Qe7 {
The passed pawn is stronger with the queen on the board} 35. Qxe7 Rxe7 36. Kg1
Kf7 37. Kf2 Kf6 38. Ke3 Ke5 39. Rd1 h5 40. Rd4 Kf5 41. Rd5+ Kg6 42. Rd4 {
Sources: The Field, 30 March 1907; Cable Match Souvenir 1926, Hoffer in The
Field} 1/2-1/2

